%I A398236 #38 Nov 12 2026 11:19:39
%S A398236 52551817,52551823,52551841,52561673,52561703,52561787,52561889,
%T A398236 52561987,52562017,52562021,52562443,52562483,52562509,52562527,
%U A398236 52562561,52562603,52562771,52565209,52565237,52566403,52566457,52566461,52567201,52567241,52567271,52567279,52567301
%N A398236 Primes p for which pi_{24;11,17}(p) - pi_{24;1,19}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A398236 From _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_: (Start)
%C A398236 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,19} and S = {11,17}, corresponding to T = {chi_{-3}, chi_24}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A398236 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_{-3}(q) + chi_24(q))/2 = 1.
%C A398236 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;11,17}(x) > pi_{24;1,19}(x) has logarithmic density 0.99496, making the race between {1,19} and {11,17} the 10th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24" below.
%C A398236 Let D(x) = pi_{24;11,17}(x) - pi_{24;1,19}(x). Below x0 = 7*10^12 there are respectively 30659637567, 30659688364, 30659698140, 30659716342, 30659718072, 30659718286, 30659752652, and 30659759379 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 44409. There are 2 sign-changing zones giving a total of 45595 terms below x0:
%C A398236   (1) a(1..27) = 52551817-52567301 (there are 605 primes a(1) <= p <= a(27) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -17 at p = 52559443);
%C A398236   (2) a(28..45595) = 6926007770491-6934323355969 (there are 205522963 primes a(28) <= p <= a(45595) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -6736 at p = 6927197854123).
%C A398236 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 205523568. (End)
%H A398236 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A398236/b398236.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..45595</a> (all terms below 7*10^12)
%H A398236 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A398236 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24159887">Sign-changing zones of prime number races between cosets modulo 24, and of pi(x;8,5) - pi(x;8,1), up to 7*10^12</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A398236 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A398236 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A398236 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A398236 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A398236 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==19, k--); if(p%24==11||p%24==17, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A398236 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A398236 nonn,changed
%O A398236 1,1
